WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.920
Aisha and Marcus find a policy phrase they understand differently.

2
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.520
Disagreement alone does not make language ambiguous.

3
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.460
Read the words in their ordinary meaning and in the context

4
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.620
of the whole policy.

5
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.480
A genuine ambiguity can exist when language reasonably supports more than

6
00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:22.380
one meaning. Interpretation rules can protect reasonable expectations and

7
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.120
resolve ambiguity, but do not assume a customer's hope overrides a

8
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.160
clear exclusion. In Maryland, courts do not simply discard clear policy

9
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.060
wording because one party expected broader protection.

10
00:00:35.922 --> 00:00:39.442
A representation is a statement offered as true to the best

11
00:00:39.442 --> 00:00:40.682
of the applicant's knowledge.

12
00:00:41.702 --> 00:00:44.442
A warranty is a stricter contractual assurance.

13
00:00:45.402 --> 00:00:49.682
Maryland law specifically treats statements by or for an insured or

14
00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:54.122
annuitant in a life or annuity application as representations, not

15
00:00:54.122 --> 00:00:57.942
warranties. That distinction does not excuse false information.

16
00:00:58.702 --> 00:01:02.562
Applicants should answer completely and accurately, and producers should

17
00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:05.282
follow the correction process when an error is found.

18
00:01:06.062 --> 00:01:09.822
Do not teach that every small error automatically voids a policy,

19
00:01:10.662 --> 00:01:13.982
or that calling an answer a representation makes it harmless.

20
00:01:14.988 --> 00:01:19.308
Under Maryland's rule, a misrepresentation, omission,

21
00:01:20.108 --> 00:01:24.288
concealment or incorrect statement can affect recovery if it is fraudulent

22
00:01:24.288 --> 00:01:27.828
or material to accepting the risk or the hazard assumed.

23
00:01:29.028 --> 00:01:33.788
The statute also addresses whether the insurer, knowing the correct facts,

24
00:01:34.468 --> 00:01:36.908
would in good faith have issued the coverage on the same

25
00:01:36.908 --> 00:01:42.248
terms. Intent to deceive is therefore not the only possible

26
00:01:42.248 --> 00:01:46.568
issue. This policy-application rule differs from the

27
00:01:46.568 --> 00:01:50.848
intentional-misrepresentation ground in a producer's license application.

28
00:01:51.808 --> 00:01:55.668
Identify which application and which rule the question actually concerns.

29
00:01:56.574 --> 00:02:00.874
The doctrine of utmost good faith describes the importance of honest

30
00:02:00.874 --> 00:02:03.134
disclosure and dealing in insurance.

31
00:02:04.274 --> 00:02:07.554
Concealment involves withholding relevant information.

32
00:02:08.514 --> 00:02:12.954
Fraud involves intentional deception and its legal elements;

33
00:02:14.274 --> 00:02:17.174
it is not another name for every innocent mistake.

34
00:02:18.114 --> 00:02:23.134
Suppose an applicant deliberately hides a requested diagnosis to obtain a

35
00:02:23.134 --> 00:02:28.334
rate. The deliberate deception raises a different issue

36
00:02:28.334 --> 00:02:30.774
from an accidental typing error.

37
00:02:31.694 --> 00:02:37.594
Both should be corrected, but consequences depend on materiality,

38
00:02:38.414 --> 00:02:43.214
the statute, policy terms and other applicable limits,

39
00:02:43.874 --> 00:02:49.034
including contestability. A producer must never supply a

40
00:02:49.034 --> 00:02:52.294
false answer to obtain more favorable terms.

41
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.440
Waiver is the voluntary relinquishment of a known right.

42
00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:00.940
Estoppel can prevent a party from taking a position inconsistent with

43
00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:04.580
conduct on which another person reasonably relied to their detriment.

44
00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:07.300
They are related but not identical.

45
00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:13.040
For example, knowingly choosing not to enforce a requirement raises a

46
00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:18.160
waiver question; misleading conduct followed by harmful reliance raises

47
00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.500
the second doctrine. Do not treat either label as a producer's

48
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:27.400
power to create coverage, override law, or erase every exclusion.

49
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.540
The available remedy depends on the facts and governing law.

50
00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:36.330
Imagine Marcus notices an incorrect answer after submission.

51
00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:42.450
Aisha should promptly follow the insurer's correction procedure, not

52
00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.530
guess that the mistake is too small to matter.

53
00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.970
The insurer can evaluate the corrected facts.

54
00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:55.610
If a later dispute arises, distinguish an interpretation issue from a

55
00:03:55.610 --> 00:04:00.890
false statement, and distinguish a contractual right from

56
00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:05.650
an excluded risk. Your exam task is to identify the relevant

57
00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:10.910
facts and doctrine. Your professional task is to prevent

58
00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:16.210
avoidable disputes through accurate records
and clear explanations from the start.
